/**

@page documentation_exahype2_solvers_aderdg ADER-DG solvers

\tableofcontents

An ADER-DG time step predicts a space-time solution in every cell, projects it onto the faces,
solves the Riemann problems of the faces and corrects the solution of every cell.

@image html documentation/exahype2/solvers/ADER-DG.png

# Data flow

The predictor forms a space-time approximation from the polynomial of a cell. It writes the
time-averaged solution and flux onto the half of every face that belongs to the cell. The Riemann
solver computes the numerical flux of a face from the traces of both halves. The corrector adds
the numerical fluxes of the faces to the cell.

For a linear PDE, the predictor integrates the face contribution in time before it projects the
contribution onto the faces. The corrector then reads time-averaged face values only.

# The time step

The solver family exahype2::dg::ader::Solver runs a time step in four steps:

1. The predictor dispatch over the owned cells writes the traces into the state and flux pools
   of the faces.
2. The halo exchange posts the halves of the exchanged faces. The local level jumps fill their
   fine faces from their coarse face during the message transfer. The Riemann dispatch over the
   local faces then writes the numerical flux of both halves.
3. The remote level jumps interpolate after the wait. The Riemann dispatch then runs over the
   remote faces.
4. The level jumps restrict the fluxes of their fine faces onto their coarse face. The halo
   exchange sends the flux halves of the exchanged faces during the corrector dispatch over the
   local cells. The corrector dispatch over the remote cells follows after the wait. The corrector
   returns the largest wave speeds for the size of the next time step.

A solver with a subcell limiter (exahype2::dg::limiting::SubcellLimiter) checks every corrected
candidate for physical admissibility and for the discrete maximum principle. It spreads the
troubled status over `NumberOfSpreadRings` rings of neighbours. The finite-volume scheme of the
limiter recomputes the limited cells on their subgrids.

# Adaptive interfaces

A hanging face couples cells on different levels. The level jump interpolates the coarse trace
onto the fine faces before the Riemann dispatch. The Riemann dispatch solves the Riemann problems
of the fine faces. The level jump restricts the numerical fluxes of the fine faces onto the coarse
face before the corrector of the coarse cell reads them. Every predictor runs once per time step.

# Coupling two solvers

`restrict_to_cells()` assigns complementary parts of the domain to two ADER-DG solvers of one
project. `project.couple(first, second)` advances both solvers with the smaller of their time
step sizes. The coupling copies the traces of the faces between their cells from the face pools
of the partner. It maps them onto the face nodes and the compute precision of the other solver.
`examples/coupling/planar_waves.py` couples a double-precision and a single-precision solver of
the acoustic wave equations.

*/
